WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.933
This is PIXIU,

00:00:04.013 --> 00:00:07.447
a comprehensive open-source framework for financial
artificial intelligence,

00:00:07.527 --> 00:00:10.960
published at NeurIPS 2023.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.473
Financial language is highly technical,

00:00:14.553 --> 00:00:17.987
yet before PIXIU there were no openly released
financial large language models,

00:00:18.067 --> 00:00:21.500
no financial instruction tuning data, and no
holistic evaluation benchmark.

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:25.013
PIXIU fills all three gaps at once.

00:00:25.093 --> 00:00:28.527
It contributes FIT, the first multi-task,

00:00:28.607 --> 00:00:32.040
multi-modal financial instruction dataset with one
hundred and thirty-six thousand samples;

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:35.553
FinMA,

00:00:35.633 --> 00:00:39.067
the first openly released instruction-following
financial large language model built by fine-tuning LLaMA;

00:00:39.147 --> 00:00:42.580
and FLARE,

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:46.093
a standardized benchmark spanning financial natural
language tasks and stock movement prediction.

00:00:46.173 --> 00:00:49.607
Together they let the community build, compare,

00:00:49.687 --> 00:00:53.120
and advance financial LLMs in the open.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:57.487
Financial technology has advanced rapidly with NLP,

00:00:57.567 --> 00:01:01.555
but the highly technical nature of financial text
demands domain-specific models.

00:01:01.635 --> 00:01:05.622
Existing financial pre-trained models like finBERT
and FLANG are small,

00:01:05.702 --> 00:01:09.689
below one billion parameters, limiting their
generalization.

00:01:09.769 --> 00:01:13.756
The one large financial model, the
fifty-billion-parameter BloombergGPT,

00:01:13.836 --> 00:01:17.824
is proprietary: neither its weights nor its
training data are released,

00:01:17.904 --> 00:01:21.891
and it is not instruction-following.

00:01:21.971 --> 00:01:25.958
Critically,

00:01:26.038 --> 00:01:30.025
there are no open financial instruction datasets
and no standardized benchmarks for comprehensively

00:01:30.105 --> 00:01:34.093
assessing financial LLMs. This leaves the research
community without the resources needed

00:01:34.173 --> 00:01:38.160
to push financial AI forward in the open.

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:42.518
Two gaps motivate this work.

00:01:42.598 --> 00:01:46.576
First,

00:01:46.656 --> 00:01:50.634
instruction tuning has proven essential for
improving a model's zero-shot ability on downstream tasks,

00:01:50.714 --> 00:01:54.692
yet no financial instruction data exists to enable it.

00:01:54.772 --> 00:01:58.750
Second,

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:02.808
existing financial benchmarks such as FLUE cover
only natural language processing tasks.

00:02:02.888 --> 00:02:06.866
They ignore financial prediction tasks like stock
movement prediction,

00:02:06.946 --> 00:02:10.924
which require exploiting both text and time-series
data and are far more aligned with real-world financial scenarios.

00:02:11.004 --> 00:02:14.982
PIXIU is built to close both gaps with open
resources, multi-task coverage,

00:02:15.062 --> 00:02:19.040
multi-modal data, and greater task diversity.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:21.502
PIXIU makes four contributions.

00:02:21.582 --> 00:02:23.246
First, it introduces FIT,

00:02:23.326 --> 00:02:28.546
the first multi-task and multi-modal instruction
tuning dataset for finance,

00:02:28.626 --> 00:02:34.335
covering five tasks and nine datasets with one
hundred thirty-six thousand samples.

00:02:34.415 --> 00:02:36.287
Second, it introduces FLARE,

00:02:36.367 --> 00:02:43.819
the first evaluation benchmark that includes both
financial language understanding and financial prediction.

00:02:43.899 --> 00:02:47.586
Third, it introduces FinMA, the first openly released,

00:02:47.666 --> 00:02:51.282
instruction-following financial large language model,

00:02:51.362 --> 00:02:56.861
achieving state-of-the-art on three financial NLP
tasks and one prediction task.

00:02:56.941 --> 00:03:00.348
Fourth, it benchmarks FinMA against existing LLMs,

00:03:00.428 --> 00:03:05.300
revealing both their superiority and their key
limitations for finance.

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:09.539
The method has three stages.

00:03:09.619 --> 00:03:13.479
First,

00:03:13.559 --> 00:03:17.418
PIXIU gathers open-released data across five
financial tasks: sentiment analysis,

00:03:17.498 --> 00:03:21.357
news headline classification, named entity
recognition, question answering,

00:03:21.437 --> 00:03:25.296
and stock movement prediction.

00:03:25.376 --> 00:03:29.236
Domain experts write diverse task-specific
instructions for each task,

00:03:29.316 --> 00:03:33.175
which are assembled with the data samples to form
the FIT instruction dataset.

00:03:33.255 --> 00:03:37.114
This data is multi-modal, spanning text, tables
from financial reports,

00:03:37.194 --> 00:03:41.054
and historical stock prices as time series.

00:03:41.134 --> 00:03:44.993
Second,

00:03:45.073 --> 00:03:48.932
LLaMA checkpoints at seven and thirty billion
parameters are fine-tuned on FIT with multi-task instruction tuning,

00:03:49.012 --> 00:03:52.871
producing the FinMA model family.

00:03:52.951 --> 00:03:56.811
Third, FinMA and other LLMs are evaluated on the
FLARE benchmark,

00:03:56.891 --> 00:04:00.750
which unifies four financial NLP tasks with six
datasets and one prediction task with three datasets.

00:04:01.130 --> 00:04:04.467
FIT, the financial instruction tuning dataset,

00:04:04.547 --> 00:04:11.672
contains one hundred thirty-six thousand
instruction samples across five tasks and nine datasets.

00:04:11.752 --> 00:04:18.209
The FLARE evaluation benchmark covers four
financial NLP tasks with six datasets and one

00:04:18.289 --> 00:04:21.626
financial prediction task with three datasets.

00:04:21.706 --> 00:04:26.232
Sentiment analysis uses the Financial Phrase Bank
and FiQA-SA,

00:04:26.312 --> 00:04:30.317
news headline classification uses the Headline dataset,

00:04:30.397 --> 00:04:34.328
named entity recognition uses a financial NER dataset,

00:04:34.408 --> 00:04:37.597
question answering uses FinQA and ConvFinQA,

00:04:37.677 --> 00:04:42.351
and stock movement prediction uses BigData22,
ACL18, and CIKM18.

00:04:42.431 --> 00:04:48.516
Each task is scored with its standard metric, such
as weighted F1, entity-level F1,

00:04:48.596 --> 00:04:54.310
exact-match accuracy, and the Matthews correlation
coefficient for prediction.

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:59.235
On the FLARE benchmark,

00:04:59.315 --> 00:05:03.861
the fine-tuned FinMA models significantly
outperform other large language models on most financial NLP tasks,

00:05:03.941 --> 00:05:08.486
including sentiment analysis, headline
classification, and named entity recognition.

00:05:08.566 --> 00:05:13.112
For example, on the Financial Phrase Bank sentiment
dataset,

00:05:13.192 --> 00:05:17.737
FinMA-30B outperforms GPT-4 by ten percent F1 and
BloombergGPT by thirty-seven percent F1.

00:05:17.817 --> 00:05:22.363
This demonstrates the value of tailoring LLMs to
the financial domain through instruction tuning.

00:05:22.443 --> 00:05:26.988
However,

00:05:27.068 --> 00:05:31.614
FinMA underperforms on question answering tasks
that demand quantitative reasoning,

00:05:31.694 --> 00:05:36.239
a limitation inherited from LLaMA's weak
mathematical ability.

00:05:36.319 --> 00:05:40.865
And across all models, stock movement prediction
remains challenging,

00:05:40.945 --> 00:05:45.490
leaving clear room for future improvement.

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:48.410
Comparing model variants is revealing.

00:05:48.490 --> 00:05:56.406
FinMA-30B shows no significantly better performance
than FinMA-7B on most NLP tasks or on stock movement prediction,

00:05:56.486 --> 00:06:03.644
indicating that the quality and diversity of the
instruction data matter more than sheer parameter count.

00:06:03.724 --> 00:06:08.745
Scale does help on complex quantitative question
answering like ConvFinQA,

00:06:08.825 --> 00:06:12.606
mirroring LLaMA's improved math ability at larger sizes,

00:06:12.686 --> 00:06:14.605
though it still trails GPT-4.

00:06:14.685 --> 00:06:19.292
Notably, FinMA-7B-full, fine-tuned on both NLP and
prediction tasks,

00:06:19.372 --> 00:06:25.152
achieves the best performance among all models on
the ACL18 stock prediction dataset,

00:06:25.232 --> 00:06:31.080
highlighting the promise of task-specific
instruction tuning for financial prediction.

00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:34.047
A few numbers capture PIXIU's impact.

00:06:34.127 --> 00:06:41.977
The FIT instruction dataset holds one hundred
thirty-six thousand samples across five tasks and nine datasets.

00:06:42.057 --> 00:06:47.600
FinMA is built on the open LLaMA model at seven and
thirty billion parameters.

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:50.772
On the Financial Phrase Bank sentiment task,

00:06:50.852 --> 00:06:56.971
FinMA-30B exceeds GPT-4 by ten percent F1 and
BloombergGPT by thirty-seven percent F1,

00:06:57.051 --> 00:07:00.792
reaching a weighted F1 around zero point eight eight.

00:07:00.872 --> 00:07:07.857
FinMA achieves state-of-the-art on three financial
NLP tasks and one financial prediction dataset,

00:07:07.937 --> 00:07:10.380
all while being fully open-sourced.

00:07:10.760 --> 00:07:12.153
The takeaway is simple.

00:07:12.233 --> 00:07:18.493
PIXIU provides the financial AI community with its
first fully open triad: an instruction-following

00:07:18.573 --> 00:07:20.478
financial large language model,

00:07:20.558 --> 00:07:25.345
a large multi-task instruction dataset, and a
holistic evaluation benchmark.

00:07:25.425 --> 00:07:30.789
It shows that careful domain instruction tuning can
let a relatively small open model

00:07:30.869 --> 00:07:35.143
beat far larger general-purpose systems on
financial language tasks,

00:07:35.223 --> 00:07:42.700
while honestly exposing quantitative reasoning and
stock movement prediction as the field's remaining open challenges.
